        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 882,
            "position_y": 26,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 916,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Replaces an array with the differences between neighbouring values:\neach value minus the one before it.\n\nIt turns positions into steps - where something WAS into how far it MOVED between samples. Applied twice it gives the change in the change: from positions, order 1 is speed and order 2 is acceleration. Use it to find where a sequence jumps, to get velocities from a recorded path, or to undo a cumsum.\n\nTHE OUTPUT IS SHORTER:\nNine values have only eight gaps between them, so the result is one value shorter than the input along the axis - and each further order loses one more. Anything expecting the original length will notice.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.diff <order: int> <axis: int>\nDefaults: order 1, axis -1 (the last axis: along each row).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.diff 2\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is differenced and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\norder (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many times to difference. Below 1 is treated as 1. If the array is not longer than the order along the axis (6 values at order 6), there is nothing left to send, and nothing is sent. Changing it does not send by itself - in the demo a metro re-sends the input five times a second.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way to difference. For a 2-D array, -1 or 1 works along each row, 0 or -2 down each column. An axis the array does not have sends nothing.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ndifferences:\nThe differences, shorter by order along the axis.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: 0 1 4 9 16 25 36 49 64 are the positions of something speeding up (the squares). Order 1 gives 1 3 5 7 9 11 13 15 - the distance covered in each step, growing steadily: the speed. Type 2 into order and you get 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 - the speed grows by the same amount every step, a constant acceleration. Order 3 gives zeros: the acceleration never changes.\n\nBottom, the inverse: 3 1 4 1 5 9 summed by np.cumsum is the path 3 4 8 9 14 23, and np.diff of that gives back 1 4 1 5 9 - the steps, all but the first, which had nothing before it to differ from. To rebuild a path from its steps exactly you also need where it started.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.cumsum is the reverse: steps back into a path. diff does the same for a STREAM of numbers, one sample at a time, and diff_filter measures movement on a smoother timescale. t.diff is the PyTorch version.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Replaces an array with the differences between neighbouring values:\neach value minus the one before it.\n\nIt turns positions into steps - where something WAS into how far it MOVED between samples. Applied twice it gives the change in the change: from positions, order 1 is speed and order 2 is acceleration. Use it to find where a sequence jumps, to get velocities from a recorded path, or to undo a cumsum.\n\nTHE OUTPUT IS SHORTER:\nNine values have only eight gaps between them, so the result is one value shorter than the input along the axis - and each further order loses one more. Anything expecting the original length will notice.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.diff <order: int> <axis: int>\nDefaults: order 1, axis -1 (the last axis: along each row).\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.diff 2\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is differenced and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\norder (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many times to difference. Below 1 is treated as 1. If the array is not longer than the order along the axis (6 values at order 6), there is nothing left to send, and nothing is sent. Changing it does not send by itself - in the demo a metro re-sends the input five times a second.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way to difference. For a 2-D array, -1 or 1 works along each row, 0 or -2 down each column. An axis the array does not have sends nothing.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ndifferences:\nThe differences, shorter by order along the axis.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: 0 1 4 9 16 25 36 49 64 are the positions of something speeding up (the squares). Order 1 gives 1 3 5 7 9 11 13 15 - the distance covered in each step, growing steadily: the speed. Type 2 into order and you get 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 - the speed grows by the same amount every step, a constant acceleration. Order 3 gives zeros: the acceleration never changes.\n\nBottom, the inverse: 3 1 4 1 5 9 summed by np.cumsum is the path 3 4 8 9 14 23, and np.diff of that gives back 1 4 1 5 9 - the steps, all but the first, which had nothing before it to differ from. To rebuild a path from its steps exactly you also need where it started.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.cumsum is the reverse: steps back into a path. diff does the same for a STREAM of numbers, one sample at a time, and diff_filter measures movement on a smoother timescale. t.diff is the PyTorch version."
        },
